# Name: Acorn/RISC OS
#
# Acorn/RISC OS 8-bit character set (Archimedes and later ARM-based Acorn
# machines) - the standard variant, an extension of ISO-8859-1 that
# redefines 0x80-0x9F (ISO-8859-1's C1 control range) with real
# printable characters; 0xA0-0xFF matches ISO-8859-1/Latin-1 exactly
# (already covered by identity, no entries needed for that range).
# Source: Wikipedia "RISC OS character set" (raw table + its own
# "Differences from ISO/IEC 8859-1" section, both cross-checked).
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RISC_OS_character_set
#
# Two bytes in the 0x80-0x8F block are deliberately NOT included:
# - 0x84: a window-control icon whose Unicode equivalent (U+1FBC0) is
#   above 0xFFFF - this file format has no astral/surrogate-pair
#   support (see komportcharset.cpp's loadCustomCharsetFile()).
# - 0x87: a combined subscript-8-superscript-7 glyph - the source
#   itself notes this was "not proposed for Unicode" and has no single
#   code point at all (it would need two separate characters glued
#   together), so it cannot be represented in this one-byte-to-one-
#   code-point format either.
# RISC OS has other documented variants (EFF, TRC, Welsh, ...) with
# slightly different 0x80-0x8F assignments - only the standard/default
# variant is covered here.
#
# Note: 0x80-0x9F is also the ISO/ECMA-48 "C1" 8-bit control-code range
# (NEL, HTS, CSI, etc. as single bytes) in some other 8-bit-terminal
# contexts - KomportEmulation does not implement any C1 control
# function today regardless of charset (every byte here already just
# draws as an invisible placeholder under "Standard"), so this table
# does not newly break any working control function; it only makes
# some of these previously-invisible bytes visible as RISC OS's own
# characters instead.
80=20AC
81=0174
82=0175
83=25F0
85=0176
86=0177
88=21E6
89=21E8
8A=21E9
8B=21E7
8C=2026
8D=2122
8E=2030
8F=2022
90=2018
91=2019
92=2039
93=203A
94=201C
95=201D
96=201E
97=2013
98=2014
99=2212
9A=0152
9B=0153
9C=2020
9D=2021
9E=FB01
9F=FB02
